---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('klaviyo')!;
const b = appBySlug('privy')!;
const path = '/compare/klaviyo-vs-privy';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/klaviyo-vs-privy');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/klaviyo-vs-privy');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Must I replace Klaviyo to use Privy displays?",
    "answer": "No. Privy documents a Klaviyo integration for display signups, properties and coupons, so capture and sending can have separate owners."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a synced phone field establish SMS eligibility?",
    "answer": "Inspect the actual subscription state and permission record; a populated field alone does not establish permission."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Privy only a popup tool?",
    "answer": "No. Privy also advertises email, SMS and automations, including abandoned-cart email reminders."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should be measured first?",
    "answer": "Verify signup routing and recovery exits separately, then compare incremental gross profit after discounts and operating costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Compare capture-only integration with a complete recovery sender change.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{label: 'Keep or improve', tool: a.name, reason: 'Price the required repair in the existing recovery workflow.'}, {label: 'Evaluate', tool: b.name, reason: "Choose display-only capture or validate Privy sending."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Decide whether to replace sending or improve capture</h2>
<p>A merchant using Klaviyo need not move recovery email to use Privy. Evaluate three concrete arrangements: Klaviyo forms and sending, Privy displays feeding Klaviyo, or Privy displays and sending together. The middle option addresses a capture problem while retaining existing flows. The last option needs proof that the required recovery branches and reporting survive the move. List growth alone does not demonstrate that a different sender recovers more orders.</p>
<h2>The capture handoff has a defined scope</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.privy.com/integrations/klaviyo">Privy’s Klaviyo integration page</a> documents syncing new display signups to a chosen list, passing custom properties and Privy-generated coupons, and enabling Klaviyo session cookies after submission. Configure the destination and display sync rules, then inspect the resulting profile. This is evidence for a capture handoff; it does not establish that a submitted phone number has the correct SMS subscription state or that a checkout event has arrived.</p>
<h2>Recovery needs an event after identity</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/35510464880027">Klaviyo’s Added to Cart guide</a> distinguishes earlier cart activity from checkout abandonment. An identified browser can enter the earlier flow, with later checkout and purchase exclusions. Its <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005080447">Shopify data reference</a> explains that checkout recovery needs a delay and a Placed Order filter because checkout events also occur for buyers. A successful Privy signup is therefore only one step in proving this workflow.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.privy.com/email">Privy’s email product page</a> includes abandoned-cart reminders as well as welcome messages. That supports evaluating Privy as a sender, rather than describing it as a popup-only product. Preview the actual account’s event, product block and return link with a changed basket before replacing a working Klaviyo checkout flow. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Proposed exercise: follow one signup to one order</h2>
<p>Submit a Privy display using a new email, note the display ID and coupon, and inspect the chosen Klaviyo list and profile. Add a product, start checkout, then purchase while reminders wait. Record the separate identity, cart, checkout and purchase timestamps. Require one intended welcome offer, the correct recovery stage and no later abandoned-cart send after purchase. Repeat with a returning subscriber, a previously unsubscribed email and a phone field without SMS permission. These are acceptance exercises, not observed conversion results.</p>
<p>Submit two displays with different custom properties to expose overwrites or accidental list routing. Use an existing subscriber who is already in a welcome flow and check re-entry. A captured address must not automatically restart every introductory sequence. Inspect confirmation and subscription status in the destination rather than treating a successful sync response as permission to send.</p>
<h2>Match the bill to the architecture</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.privy.com/pricing">Privy’s current pricing page</a> separates email plans based on mailable contacts from display-only plans based on monthly page views. It lists email starting at $30 per month with unlimited sends and display-only starting at $24 per month, plus an SMS add-on. Compare the relevant usage tier, not just these starting prices. The page offers a trial; a trial is not an ongoing free sending plan.</p>
<p>For Privy displays plus Klaviyo, add the display bill to the existing sender bill and the time spent maintaining the handoff. For a complete sender move, include message rebuilding, deliverability setup, reporting changes and subscriber reconciliation. For Klaviyo alone, price repairing the current forms first. Use equivalent mailable audiences and traffic months; a high-traffic store with few email contacts can rank these options differently from a repeat-purchase store with modest traffic.</p>
<h2>Coupon cost can exceed capture software cost</h2>
<p>In the original hypothetical $476 basket at 40% gross margin, fixed goods cost is $285.60. Full-price gross profit is $190.40; a 16% code produces $399.84 revenue and $114.24 gross profit. The code costs $76.16 per order. One hundred such discounted orders forgo $7,616 of gross profit relative to full price, before any added orders are credited. Do not confuse that comparison with an observed loss caused by a particular form.</p>
<p>For the $631 basket at 43% margin, fixed goods cost is $359.67 and full-price gross profit is $271.33. A 20% discount leaves $504.80 revenue and $145.13 gross profit, costing $126.20. Both examples hold goods cost fixed and exclude shipping, payment fees, fulfillment, returns, tax and software. Compare the incremental orders a capture offer creates with its discount cost across all redeemers, including people who would have bought anyway.</p>
<h2>Keep the signup promise through checkout</h2>
<p>If a Privy form gives a welcome coupon, pass that same offer into the intended message and inspect expiration and stacking at checkout. Do not promise a second recovery discount automatically. For bundle pricing, check that the recovery link preserves the bundle instead of adding an incompatible code. For subscription toggles, state recurring terms clearly; for gift purchases, verify the gift message survives. A repeat customer should not receive a misleading new-customer offer.</p>
<p>On mobile, make sure the display can be dismissed and the recovery destination restores the selected size and quantity. If variants are out of stock, suppress the unavailable promise or offer an honest alternative. Resolve MOQ restrictions with quantity guidance, shipping shock with delivery-cost information, and financing/BNPL questions with accurate payment help. These are different reasons to abandon; copying the same incentive across them hides whether the form, checkout or offer needs repair.</p>
<h2>Migrate forms and sending as separate releases</h2>
<p>When retaining Klaviyo, move one display first and compare captured identities, list routing, coupons and subscription states before expanding. When moving sending to Privy, inventory Shopify messages and every Klaviyo cart, checkout and welcome flow. Preserve opt-outs and permission provenance, export historical reports, and decide who finishes pending carts. Pause the former owner before enabling the replacement and keep a ledger of already sent reminders to prevent replay.</p>
<p>Stop new enrollment if list routing, coupon rendering or purchase exits fail. Reconcile pending queues before rollback. Evaluate capture rate separately from incremental recovery: more signups can increase flow volume without improving profit per visitor. Compare matched traffic and order IDs, avoid adding both senders’ attribution totals, and use a holdout large enough for the store’s order volume rather than prescribing a universal 5% sample.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
